## Rethinking Industrial Artificial Intelligence:
A Unified Foundation Framework

Authors:
Jay Lee, Hanqi Su

Year:
2025

arXiv:
https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.01797

PDF:
https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.01797.pdf

### Summary

The paper argues that Industrial AI has focused
too heavily on algorithms and models while
underestimating the importance of domain knowledge.

To address this gap, the authors propose a unified
Industrial AI Foundation Framework consisting of:

- Knowledge Module
- Data Module
- Model Module

The framework aims to provide a more systematic
foundation for Industrial AI applications.

### Key Concepts

- Knowledge-Centric AI
- Industrial AI Foundation
- Domain Knowledge
- Data Integration
- Model Integration

### Industrial AI Relevance

Industrial systems operate in environments
governed by physical constraints, engineering rules,
and operational context.

The paper emphasizes that models alone are insufficient.

Domain knowledge must be captured and integrated
as a first-class component of Industrial AI systems.

### Industrial AI Lens

✔ Knowledge is as important as models

✔ Industrial AI requires domain understanding

✔ Data without context has limited value

✔ Physical systems require engineering constraints

### Relation to Cognitive Digital Twins

The framework supports the idea that intelligent
digital twins should combine:

- Data
- Domain Knowledge
- Models
- Context

rather than relying on predictive models alone.

### Relation to Industrial Decision Intelligence Architecture (IDIA)

Data Layer
↓
Knowledge Layer

Observation:

The paper provides strong support for the
Knowledge Layer concept.

However, it does not extend into decision-making,
governance, authority management, or autonomous execution.
